WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
A manager who can't work with AI agents

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.300
is in exactly the same position.

3
00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:05.229
Imagine a manager who can't work with

4
00:00:05.229 --> 00:00:05.549
people.

5
00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:06.689
Who can't delegate.

6
00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:08.589
Who doesn't understand the processes in their

7
00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:09.169
own area.

8
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:10.429
Would you hire that manager?

9
00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:11.329
Probably not.

10
00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:12.669
Now I'm going to say something that

11
00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:13.889
might make you uncomfortable.

12
00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:14.649
From now on,

13
00:00:14.649 --> 00:00:16.229
a manager who can't work with AI

14
00:00:16.229 --> 00:00:17.949
agents is in exactly the same position.

15
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:19.369
They just haven't realized it yet.

16
00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:21.489
For decades, management meant taking care of

17
00:00:21.489 --> 00:00:23.669
two things: people and processes.

18
00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:25.029
You learned how to hire,

19
00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:26.669
delegate, give feedback,

20
00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:28.389
and hold people accountable for results.

21
00:00:28.389 --> 00:00:30.029
You learned how to map a workflow,

22
00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.129
find the bottleneck,

23
00:00:31.129 --> 00:00:32.109
and improve the process.

24
00:00:32.109 --> 00:00:33.689
Now management has a third thing to

25
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:33.989
manage.

26
00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:35.449
Alongside people and processes:

27
00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:36.029
agents.

28
00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:37.869
And this isn't a technical detail.

29
00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:39.149
It's part of your job.

30
00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:40.629
I know the first reaction.

31
00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:42.809
“That's IT's job.” “I have people for

32
00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.129
that.” So let me ask you:

33
00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:45.809
do you also have someone give your

34
00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:46.689
team feedback for you?

35
00:00:46.689 --> 00:00:48.769
Decide your area's priorities for you?

36
00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:49.589
No, right?

37
00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:50.789
Because that's management.

38
00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:52.609
And managing agents is management too.

39
00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:54.669
Who will decide what an agent can

40
00:00:54.669 --> 00:00:56.129
do on its own and what needs

41
00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:56.769
your approval?

42
00:00:56.769 --> 00:00:58.209
Who will say whether the result is

43
00:00:58.209 --> 00:00:58.349
good?

44
00:00:58.349 --> 00:00:59.849
If you don't decide,

45
00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:01.169
someone else will decide for you.

46
00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:02.809
And that person might not even understand

47
00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:03.549
your business.

48
00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:05.449
And here's something few people talk about.

49
00:01:05.449 --> 00:01:07.629
Your team is probably already using AI.

50
00:01:07.629 --> 00:01:08.829
Maybe on their phones,

51
00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:09.869
maybe in secret,

52
00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.289
maybe with no rules at all.

53
00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:13.149
Your analyst gets in ten minutes what

54
00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:14.309
used to take an afternoon.

55
00:01:14.309 --> 00:01:15.889
Your intern puts together a report,

56
00:01:15.889 --> 00:01:17.449
and you don't even know how it

57
00:01:17.449 --> 00:01:17.989
was made.

58
00:01:17.989 --> 00:01:19.209
And as their manager,

59
00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:20.509
you don't know what goes in,

60
00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:21.089
what comes out,

61
00:01:21.089 --> 00:01:22.069
or where the risk is.

62
00:01:22.069 --> 00:01:23.409
That's not a lack of technology.

63
00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:24.629
It's a lack of management.

64
00:01:24.629 --> 00:01:25.729
Creating an agent is easy.

65
00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:26.869
Managing one isn't.

66
00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:28.829
A manager who coordinates ten people could

67
00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:30.469
end up coordinating ten people and dozens

68
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:31.029
of agents.

69
00:01:31.029 --> 00:01:32.529
But a poorly managed agent doesn't go

70
00:01:32.529 --> 00:01:32.809
away.

71
00:01:32.809 --> 00:01:34.049
It multiplies mistakes.

72
00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:35.429
It does the wrong thing faster,

73
00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:36.409
in more places,

74
00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:37.069
all at once.

75
00:01:37.069 --> 00:01:38.329
And the worst process of all is

76
00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:39.789
a bad process sped up by AI.

77
00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:41.129
You can't just drop an agent into

78
00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:41.589
a mess.

79
00:01:41.589 --> 00:01:43.249
First, you need to understand the process.

80
00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:44.929
Then decide where a person fits,

81
00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:45.729
where an agent fits,

82
00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:46.869
and where they work together.

83
00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:47.729
The good news is,

84
00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.289
you already know a lot of this.

85
00:01:49.289 --> 00:01:50.849
Managing agents has a lot in common

86
00:01:50.849 --> 00:01:51.769
with managing people.

87
00:01:51.769 --> 00:01:52.689
Clear goals.

88
00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:53.449
Context.

89
00:01:53.449 --> 00:01:54.009
Tools.

90
00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:55.149
Limits on autonomy.

91
00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:56.009
Evaluation.

92
00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:56.649
Feedback.

93
00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:57.889
But with more control:

94
00:01:57.889 --> 00:01:59.429
you can see exactly what the agent

95
00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:00.709
did, step by step.

96
00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:02.509
And good management rules still apply.

97
00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:04.089
The person doing the work doesn't approve

98
00:02:04.089 --> 00:02:04.929
their own work.

99
00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:05.989
The agent prepares it;

100
00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:07.609
the person decides and signs off.

101
00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:09.289
And some decisions are outside the agent's

102
00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:09.809
authority.

103
00:02:09.809 --> 00:02:11.009
Who sets these rules?

104
00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:11.749
The manager.

105
00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:12.809
But there's a catch.

106
00:02:12.809 --> 00:02:14.209
You don't learn to manage agents by

107
00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:14.909
reading about them.

108
00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:16.289
Just like nobody learned to lead by

109
00:02:16.289 --> 00:02:17.549
reading leadership books alone.

110
00:02:17.549 --> 00:02:18.589
You have to use them.

111
00:02:18.589 --> 00:02:19.769
Create an agent.

112
00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:21.109
Watch it make mistakes.

113
00:02:21.109 --> 00:02:22.209
Understand why.

114
00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:23.969
And that's where something that scares a

115
00:02:23.969 --> 00:02:25.089
lot of managers comes in:

116
00:02:25.089 --> 00:02:25.809
the terminal.

117
00:02:25.809 --> 00:02:27.329
Today, the terminal is one of the

118
00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:29.029
most powerful ways to work with agents.

119
00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:29.549
And I know:

120
00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:30.709
black screen, commands.

121
00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:32.369
It looks like something for programmers.

122
00:02:32.369 --> 00:02:34.196
That's why we created OSWork at INEMA

123
00:02:34.196 --> 00:02:34.869
dot club.

124
00:02:34.869 --> 00:02:36.549
It's a starting point designed for people

125
00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:37.769
who've never done this before.

126
00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:38.949
You open the terminal,

127
00:02:38.949 --> 00:02:40.289
create your project folder,

128
00:02:40.289 --> 00:02:41.749
see everything in File Explorer,

129
00:02:41.749 --> 00:02:43.129
put an agent to work in that

130
00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.809
folder, and even publish your work.

131
00:02:44.809 --> 00:02:45.789
Step by step.

132
00:02:45.789 --> 00:02:47.289
The goal isn't to turn you into

133
00:02:47.289 --> 00:02:47.889
a programmer.

134
00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:49.229
It's to take the fear out of

135
00:02:49.229 --> 00:02:50.569
the environment where agents work.

136
00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:52.449
After OSWork comes the part that's really

137
00:02:52.449 --> 00:02:53.789
yours: management.

138
00:02:53.789 --> 00:02:54.989
At INEMA dot club,

139
00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:56.609
you'll find the Agent Management course and

140
00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.589
its seven principles:

141
00:02:57.589 --> 00:02:59.249
intent, context, data,

142
00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:01.469
success criteria, autonomy with limits,

143
00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:02.929
observation, and supervision.

144
00:03:02.929 --> 00:03:04.809
And in INEMA's AI Management area for

145
00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:06.529
events, we cover the whole picture:

146
00:03:06.529 --> 00:03:08.829
strategy, delegation, orchestration,

147
00:03:08.829 --> 00:03:10.529
governance, and evaluation.

148
00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:11.629
It's all open and free.

149
00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:13.509
So the question isn't whether you'll need

150
00:03:13.509 --> 00:03:14.449
to manage agents.

151
00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:15.349
The question is:

152
00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.809
when that lands on your desk,

153
00:03:16.809 --> 00:03:17.869
will you know what to do,

154
00:03:17.869 --> 00:03:19.329
or will you depend on someone who

155
00:03:19.329 --> 00:03:19.689
does?

156
00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:20.989
You still have time to learn at

157
00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:22.749
your own pace and make mistakes without

158
00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:23.229
pressure.

159
00:03:23.229 --> 00:03:25.169
Before long, a manager who can't manage

160
00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:26.729
agents will be seen the same way

161
00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:28.369
as one who can't work with people.

162
00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:30.389
The manager of the future manages people,

163
00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:31.669
processes, and agents.

164
00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:32.889
Start with OSWork.

165
00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:33.769
Then management.

166
00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:35.042
And start this week.

